title: The Works Councils Law
subtitle: The law the dead protested against is in force.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1920 and month = 2
max-visits: 1
priority: 2

= The Works Councils Law

On 4 February the Works Councils Law comes into force. In every firm with twenty workers or more, the staff will elect a council. It may inspect the balance sheet and speak on dismissals. It may not strike, and it must keep the peace in the works.

The unions mean to fill the councils with their own men. In Berlin, Richard Müller and Ernst Däumig of the USPD left want to link the councils into a central office of their own, beyond what the law allows. [? if massacre_response = 4 : We told the workers in January to boycott the councils. Many are asking whether that still holds. ?]

The elections begin this spring, factory by factory.

- @stand: Stand communist lists in every council election.
- @fractions: Stand in the elections, and build communist cells in the unions.
- @network: Link the councils into a revolutionary network, whatever the law says.
- @aau: Leave the old unions for revolutionary factory organisations.

@stand
subtitle: The Spartacist line: wherever workers vote, communists must stand.
on-arrival: {!
Q.works_councils_line = 1;
Q.worker_support += 3;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 6;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 2;
if (Q.massacre_response == 4) {
    Q.ultraleft_satisfaction -= 6;
    Q.unity -= 3;
}
!}

Our lists win a few hundred council seats in the first month, in the machine shops of Berlin, the mines of Halle-Merseburg and the shipyards of the coast.

@fractions
subtitle: [? if lock_union_work = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] The Right's line: the councils and the unions together are where the masses are.
choose-if: lock_union_work < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids work inside the reformist unions.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_union_work == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.works_councils_line = 2;
Q.worker_support += 4;
Q.district_saxony_members += 0.5;
Q.district_thuringia_members += 0.3;
Q.unionist_satisfaction += 8;
Q.ultraleft_satisfaction -= 6;
Q.lean_unionist += 2;
!}

In Saxony and Thuringia, Brandler's people stand for the councils and for every union post that falls vacant. The union bosses complain of communist infiltration. The members re-elect our men anyway.

@network
subtitle: [? if lock_councils = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] Draws the USPD left closer. The employers and the police will call it sedition.
choose-if: lock_councils < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids it.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_councils == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.works_councils_line = 3;
Q.militancy += 4;
Q.repression += 3;
Q.uspd_relation += 6;
Q.uspd_score += 1;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 5;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 3;
Q.lean_offensive += 1;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
!}

With Müller and Däumig, our council members set up a Berlin centre of works councils. It meets in a hall in Neukölln, and its resolutions go far beyond the law.

@aau
subtitle: The Left Radical line. Splits the factory floor; delights Hamburg and Bremen.
view-if: ultraleft_gone = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.works_councils_line = 4;
Q.militancy += 5;
Q.worker_support -= 3;
Q.ultraleft_satisfaction += 12;
Q.unionist_satisfaction -= 12;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction -= 5;
Q.district_hamburg_loyalty += 5;
Q.district_bremen_loyalty += 5;
Q.lean_ultraleft += 3;
!}

Our militants tear up their union cards and found factory organisations that will answer to no union and no parliament. In the shipyards they are a power. Elsewhere, they are a sect.
